UID:fc896e87e7306240@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260908T210759Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Annual Tea Celebrating Deaf Culture to Take P
 lace September 17
DESCRIPTION:Skip First Level Navigation\nSkip All Navigation\nMaine.gov\nA
 gencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp\n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nMaine Departmen
 t of Labor\nContact Us\n|\nNews\n|\nOnline services\n|\nPublications\n|\nS
 itemap\nHome\nContact Us\nOnline Services\nAbout MDOL\nNews & Events\nForm
 s & Publications\nMDOL Bureaus\nHome\n→\nNews & Events\n→ Annual Tea C
 elebrating Deaf Culture to Take Place September 17\nServices\nJobs & Train
 ing\nUnemployment Insurance\nDisability Services\nWorkplace Safety\nLabor 
 Laws\nLabor Statistics\nWorkforce Development\nAnnual Tea Celebrating Deaf
  Culture to Take Place September 17\nSeptember 8\, 2026\nFOR IMMEDIATE REL
 EASE: September 8\, 2026\nContact: Terry Morrell 470-7668 (videophone)\; 7
 11 Maine Relay\nAnnual Tea Celebrating Deaf Culture to Take Place Septembe
 r 17\nThe 35th annual tea and awards presentation will take place from 2 t
 o 4 p.m. in the Hall of Flags\nAUGUSTA\, MAINE\n-In celebration of Deaf cu
 lture and the heritage of AmericanSign Language (ASL)\, as well as Deaf li
 terature\, art and folklore\, Governor Janet Mills has issued a\nproclamat
 ion\ndesignating the week of Sept. 20-26\, 2026\, as Deaf Culture Week.\nT
 o kick off the week\, the Commission for the Deaf\, Hard of Hearing and La
 te Deafened will hold its\n35th Annual Tea in Celebration of Deaf Culture 
 Week on Sept. 17\, from 2 to 4 p.m. at the Hall of Flags in the State Hous
 e in Augusta.\nThe event is open to the public.\nClayton Marr III will giv
 e the keynote address. Marr grew up in Westbrook and currently lives in Kn
 oxville\, Tenn. where he is the student activity coordinator for the\nTenn
 essee Schools for the Deaf\n. He works with Deaf students to support their
  academic success\, personal growth\, and independence.\n"Maine's Deaf com
 munity has enriched our state for generations through its language\, cultu
 re\, traditions and contributions. I've proudly declared September 20-26 a
 s Deaf Culture Week in celebration of that heritage and the Deaf and hard-
 of-hearing Mainers who make our state a better place\," said Governor Mill
 s.\n"The Maine Dep\nhttp://www.maine.gov/labor/news_events/article.shtml?i
 d=13362246
URL:http://www.maine.gov/labor/news_events/article.shtml?id=13362246
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:16ea652dda9957c7@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260907T213043Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] This Labor Day\, the US Department of Labor c
 elebrates 250 years of the American worker\, our nation’s greatest asset
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – As America celebrates Labor Day and its 250th a
 nniversary\, the U.S. Department of Labor is honoring the generations of A
 merican workers whose skill\, strength\, ingenuity\, and determination bui
 lt the greatest nation and most powerful economy the world has ever known.
 For 250 years\, American workers have built our cities\, cultivated our la
 nd\, powered our factories\, forged our steel\, laid our railroads\, pione
 ered new technologies\, and carried American industry to every corner of t
 he globe. From the workshops and farms of 1776 to the factories\, construc
 tion sites\, small businesses\, and cutting-edge industries of today\, the
  American worker has always been the backbone of our nation.“American wo
 rkers are the greatest workers in the world\,” said Acting Secretary of 
 Labor Keith Sonderling. “For 250 years\, their skill\, ingenuity\, deter
 mination\, and hard work have built the greatest nation and most powerful 
 economy the world has ever known. This Labor Day\, we celebrate the Ameri
 can workers\, job creators\, retirees\, and unions who have built\, manufa
 ctured\, invented\, and always outwork the rest of the world and who will 
 lead America into its next 250 years.”Today\, more Americans are working
  than at any point in the nation’s history. The unemployment rate remain
 s below the historic average. The economy has added more than 1 million pr
 ivate sector jobs since President Trump took office\, 401(k)s have reached
  record highs\, and the U.S. manufacturing sector has expanded for eight s
 traight months.President Trump has secured trillions of dollars in new inv
 estments\, bringing jobs and high-skilled\, high-paying opportunities back
  to communities across the country. At the Department of Labor\, we are en
 suring American workers have the skills to fill those jobs by expanding ap
 prenticeships and skills-based training and preparing the next generation 
 for the good-paying careers these investments are creating.This Labor Day\
 , we celebrate the American workers and job creators who buil\nhttp://www.
 dol.gov/newsroom/releases/dol/dol20260907
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/dol/dol20260907
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
