BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:5e060c0d6ad4bd8c@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260828T221753Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260827
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Killam v. MaineHealth
DESCRIPTION:STATE OF MAINE  APPELLATE DIVISON \nWORKERS’ COMPENSATION BO
 ARD  Case No. App. Div. 25-0013 \n  Decision No. 26-08 \n \n \nTAMMIE L. (
 COOMBS) KILLAM \n(Appellee) \n \nv. \n \nMAINEHEALTH \n(Appellant) \n \nan
 d \n \nSYNERNET \n(Insurer) \n \nConference held:  April 8\, 2026  \nDecid
 ed:  August 27\, 2026 \n \nPANEL MEMBERS: Administrative Law Judges Stoval
 l\, Rooks\, and Smith \nBy: Administrative Law Judge Smith \n \n[¶1]  Mai
 neHealth/Maine Coast Memorial Hospital (the Hospital) appeals \nfrom a dec
 ision of a Workers’ Compensation Board administrative law judge (Hirtle\
 , \nALJ) denying the Hospital’s Petition for Review pertaining to Tammie
  Killam’s July \n2\, 2014 \, work injury. The Hospital co ntends the ALJ
  erred by interpreting the \nindependent medical examiner’s (IME’S) op
 inion to contain an “unambiguous” \nassessment of no work capacity \, 
 and by adopting that interpretation .  We disagree \nand affirm the decisi
 on.  \nI.  BACKGROUND \n[¶2]  Tammie Killam was hired by the Hospital as 
 a full -time orderly on \nOctober 21\, 2013.  On July 2\, 2014\, while mov
 ing a table in the operating room\, she \n2 \n \nfelt and heard a pop on t
 he ulnar side of her let wrist. As established in a consent \ndecree dated
  March 7\, 2018 \, Ms. Killam sustained a work-related left wrist injury \
 non July 2\, 2014\, and resulting “psychological overlay consistent with
  an adjustment \ndisorder with depression and anxiety.” The parties agre
 ed she was entitled to receive \ntotal incapacity benefits without prejudi
 ce\, pursuant to 39-A M.R.S.A. § 212.   \n[¶3]  In 2020\, the Hospital f
 iled a Petition for Review and sought to reduce \nMs. Killam’s level of 
 benefits to reflect a  full-time\, minimum wage -earning \ncapacity. Ms. K
 illam then filed a Petition for Payment of Medical and Related \nServices.
  The ALJ found in a decree dated August 11\, 2022\, that Ms. Killam had \n
 developed causally related symptoms and limitations in her right wrist and
  remained \ntotally incapacitated to earn because of her work-related inju
 ry and its psychological \nsequela\nhttps://www.maine.gov/wcb/Departments/
 appellate/2026decisions/26-8_Killam_v._MaineHealth_8-27-26.pdf
URL:https://www.maine.gov/wcb/Departments/appellate/2026decisions/26-8_Kil
 lam_v._MaineHealth_8-27-26.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f37c2360d3c15ff5@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260828T221635Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine’s Annual Hire-A-Vet Campaign
DESCRIPTION:Hello\, this is Governor Janet Mills\, and thank you for liste
 ning.\nYou know\, one of the great privileges of serving as your Governor 
 is working with so many veterans to make sure they have all the opportunit
 ies that they deserve in our state. Our state is proud that we have one of
  the highest number of veterans per capita of any state in the country. An
 d we're here to serve them.\nI am proud to have the benefit of a number of
  veterans in my Administration\, including members of the Cabinet like Com
 missioner Randy Liberty\, Commissioner Mike Sauschuck\, Commissioner and A
 djutant General Diane Dunn\, as well as a wonderful group of Aide de Camp 
 who advised me and my Administration on issues relating to veterans.\nI al
 so come from a proud military family. Two uncles who fought in the Pacific
  in World War II. My brother who served three tours of duty in Vietnam\, a
 nd my father who served in the Pacific during World War II at the Battle o
 f Leyte Gulf. So I know that when our friends and family members come home
  from serving on the front lines\, they deserve every opportunity to find 
 rewarding careers in the civil sector that utilize their extensive skills\
 , diverse experiences\, and their unique training.\nMaine's Hire-A-Vet Cam
 paign\nis a key part of expanding those opportunities. The campaign had am
 bitious goals last year: 100 days\, 100 employers\, and 100 veterans hired
 . And thanks to everyone’s hard work\, we achieved those goals\, and we 
 place veterans in good-paying jobs in every county in Maine. The employers
  hiring the most veterans included the State of Maine itself\, Bonney Staf
 fing\, Northern Light Health\, and Bath Iron Works.\nSince its inception i
 n 2015\, the Hire-A-Vet Campaign has connected more than 2\,000 employers 
 with over 2\,200 veterans hired. That's incredible.\nAnd to all the busine
 sses who helped make that happen\, I say thank you. You folks hired Maine 
 veterans because you recognized what we all know to be true. Veterans know
  what it means to work as a team and to leave\nhttp://www.maine.gov/govern
 or/mills/news/radio_address/maines-annual-hire-vet-campaign-2026-08-28
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/maines-annual-h
 ire-vet-campaign-2026-08-28
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:8b5ccf0d05031a54@role-employment-labor.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260828T221633Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Adult Education and Family Literacy Week - Se
 ptember 13 through 19
DESCRIPTION:WHEREAS\n\, thousands of adults in Maine need assistance to qu
 alify for better-paying or more fulfilling jobs\, to achieve a post-second
 ary education\, or to improve their English language skills\; and\nWHEREAS
 \n\, success in these endeavors leads to these adults becoming more effect
 ive family members\, citizens\, and workers\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, Maine has o
 ver 65 adult education programs to assist residents seeking to become job-
 ready\, advance in the workplace\, prepare for a post-secondary education\
 , or increase their literacy in the English language\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, ad
 ult education can often provide this assistance in a more cost-effective m
 anner than other educational alternatives through a decentralized system t
 hat is within financial and geographic reach of anyone in the State of Mai
 ne\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, adult education students vary in age\, background\, 
 ethnicity\, and aspirations\; and\nWHEREAS\n\, Maine is dedicated to helpi
 ng these students achieve the skills they need to expand their future care
 er and educational opportunities\;\nNOW\, THEREFORE\n\, be it resolved tha
 t I\, Janet T. Mills\, Governor of the State of Maine\, do hereby proclaim
  the week of September 13-19\, 2026 as\nAdult Education and Family Literac
 y Week\nthroughout the State of Maine\, and I urge all citizens to recogni
 ze this observance.\nIn testimony whereof\, I have caused\nthe Great Seal 
 of the State to be\nhereunto affixed GIVEN under my\nhand at Augusta this 
 twenty seventh day\nof August Two Thousand Twenty-Six\nhttp://www.maine.go
 v/governor/mills/official_documents/proclamations/2026-08-adult-education-
 and-family-literacy-week-september-13
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/official_documents/proclamations/2
 026-08-adult-education-and-family-literacy-week-september-13
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
